<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Corinna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Corinna of Tanagra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Corinna was a lyric poet from Tanagra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> who wrote narrative poems on local myths in the Boeotian dialect. Ancient tradition made her a contemporary and rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a>, said to have beaten him in competition and advised him on the use of myth, but many modern scholars date her to the third century BCE. Fragments of her work survive on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corinna.jpg" alt="Corinna" width="320" height="492" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frederic Leighton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leighton_corinna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
